require "dma/pil/dma.pil"
require "dma/pil/dma_loop.pil"
require "dma/pil/dma_with_pre_post.pil"

/*
    DmaCompact
    ==========

    Two DMA airs inlined into a single one:

      · the controller with its PRE/POST sub-operations -- `DmaWithPrePost`
                                                            (precompiles/dma/pil/dma_with_pre_post.pil)
      · the 64-bit loop, aligned or not                  -- `DmaLoop`
                                                            (precompiles/dma/pil/dma_loop.pil)

    EVERY row carries the two of them at once: the `wpp_` block is one row of the `DmaWithPrePost`
    sequence and the `loop_` block one row of the `DmaLoop` sequence. Each block keeps its own
    columns and its own buses, the blocks share nothing but the row index, and no constraint
    crosses from one block to the other. Read this air as the two of them stacked side by side.

        ROW i   ┌──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┐
                │ wpp_*   : one row of the `DmaWithPrePost` sequence               │
                │ loop_*  : one row of the `DmaLoop` sequence (loop_op_x_row words) │
                └──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┘

    Why
    ───

    Every DMA operation that is not direct needs a controller row, and every operation with at least
    one whole 64-bit word needs loop rows, so an execution that uses the DMA at all opens a
    `DmaWithPrePost` instance plus one loop instance (a `Dma64Aligned*` one, a `DmaUnaligned` one, or
    both). When the DMA is used lightly all of them are far from full, and what costs is not the basic
    proof but the recursion over the number of instances. One instance of this air does the work of
    two or three.

    How
    ───

    The two airtemplates are expanded here with `inline`, which declares their columns, air values
    and constraints on this air with the alias as prefix. Nothing is transcribed: the constraints
    and the bus interactions of each block ARE the ones of the airtemplate it comes from, so the
    soundness argument is theirs (see the two files above) and the witness is computed by their own
    code, through the row adapters of `precompiles/dma/src/dma_block_rows.rs`.

    The bus interactions differ in shape but not in content: the two blocks share one gsum
    accumulator, so the standard library packs their terms into different clusters than it does when
    each air accumulates on its own.

    Operations do not cross the blocks: the controller of the `wpp_` block delegates its loop on the
    operation bus exactly as `DmaWithPrePost` does, and whichever loop air the planner routed that
    loop to proves it -- the `loop_` block of this same instance, or any other.

    Continuations
    ─────────────

    The `wpp_` block has none: an operation is atomic there (its extra PRE row must follow its DMA
    row), so every instance proves whole operations and nothing flows from one to the next.

    The `loop_` block keeps the continuation chain of `DmaLoop` on DMA_LOOP_CONT_ID, with a
    `cont_subid` of its own taken from the same `proof.dma_loop` container, so its chain never mixes
    with the one of a standalone `DmaLoop`. The instances of this air are the segments of that chain,
    and `loop_enable_flag` gates its global seed exactly as `enable_flag` does in `DmaLoop`: it has to
    be on whenever this air has an instance, even when its `loop_` block is only padding.
*/

airtemplate DmaCompact(int N = 2**20,
                       const expr loop_enable_flag = 1,   // gates the global seed of the loop chain
                       const int loop_op_x_row = 4,       // 64-bit words per row of the loop block
                       int loop_cont_subid = 0) {         // subid of the loop chain, 0 = auto

    // BLOCK 1 of 2 -- the controller with its PRE/POST sub-operations
    inline DmaWithPrePost(N: N) alias wpp_;

    // BLOCK 2 of 2 -- the loop, aligned or not
    inline DmaLoop(N: N, enable_flag: loop_enable_flag, op_x_row: loop_op_x_row,
                   cont_subid: loop_cont_subid) alias loop_;
}
